# ~/.monitor.env in the home directory of the account the cron entry runs as,
# mode 0600.
#
# monitor.sh sources this, so deploying the script is a plain copy with nothing
# hand-patched. Nothing here is in the repository with a real value.

# Verify this delivers before you rely on it. Discord answers HTTP 204 on
# success, and monitor.sh reports any other code to its own log, because a
# revoked or mistyped webhook fails invisibly and the first anyone knows is an
# outage nobody was paged for:
#
#   curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\n' -H 'content-type: application/json' \
#     -d '{"content":"monitor test"}' "$DISCORD_WEBHOOK_URL"
DISCORD_WEBHOOK_URL=

# Mandatory. monitor.sh sources this file before it resolves a single default
# and then exits 2 while MONITOR_DOMAIN still contains angle brackets, so an
# unedited env file stops the monitor loudly rather than probing a hostname
# that cannot resolve and latching an alert on it.
#
# **Every placeholder below is quoted, and a value that contains `<` or `>`
# has to stay quoted.** This file is sourced by a shell, where `X=<domain>` is
# an assignment followed by two redirection operators: bash reports a syntax
# error and stops reading the file THERE, so every setting after the offending
# line silently never reaches the script. An operator who filled in
# MONITOR_DOMAIN and MONITOR_EXPECT_GENESIS but left MONITOR_SSL_DIR as shipped
# would lose the genesis hash that way, and the check that catches a node which
# resynced from the spec would be skipped with no message at all. monitor.sh
# now refuses to run when the source fails, which is the other half of this.
MONITOR_DOMAIN="<domain>"
# Derived from the line above on purpose, so filling in one fills in both and
# there is no second place to forget.
MONITOR_SSL_DIR="$HOME/ssl/$MONITOR_DOMAIN"

# The genesis hash this deployment launched, from the node's startup line
# ("Initialized genesis block 0x..."). A node that resynced from a different
# spec answers happily and serves an empty tree, so this is what catches it.
MONITOR_EXPECT_GENESIS=

# An optional extra floor, and nothing has to be raised by hand any more.
# monitor.sh remembers the best height it has ever seen in its state directory
# and alerts when the head falls more than MONITOR_HEIGHT_DROP below it, which
# is what catches a node that lost its database and resynced from the spec:
# that node answers every call happily and serves both wallets an empty tree.
# Leave this at 1, or set it to a height this deployment is known to be past if
# an absolute floor is wanted as well. The floor is the one case the memory
# cannot cover: a monitor whose state directory is as new as the resynced node.
MONITOR_MIN_HEIGHT=1

# How far the head may fall below that remembered best before it is a fall
# rather than a reorg. A node that resynced from the spec goes to zero; a reorg
# on this chain is a block or two. After a deliberate chain replacement, delete
# $MONITOR_STATE_DIR/height and set the new MONITOR_EXPECT_GENESIS, or this
# alerts on the new chain for as long as it is below the old one's height.
MONITOR_HEIGHT_DROP=100

# Fifteen block intervals at the 120 s target. A tip that has not moved for
# this long is the node stopped, and the alert carries the peer count with it,
# because a stall with peers and a stall without them are different problems.
MONITOR_STALE_SECS=1800

# State outside /tmp, deliberately: a reboot wipes /tmp and every active alert
# then re-fires as new.
MONITOR_STATE_DIR="$HOME/monitor-state"
MONITOR_DEBOUNCE=2
